.TH TANGSIBLE-RERUN 1 "August 2026" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.SH NAME
tangsible-rerun \- reopen the rerun dialog from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tangsible rerun
.RI [ "playbook.yml or role name" ]
.RI [ \-\-start\-at\-play
.IR name ]
.RI [ "ansible\-playbook args" ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rerun
is the same live tree UI as
.BR tangsible-run (1),
except it starts with the rerun dialog already open instead of an
already\-running playbook, pre\-filled from the most recent matching
invocation recorded in
.BR .tangsible/state.toml .
Confirming the dialog (Enter) starts the run exactly as
.B tangsible run
would have.
.PP
Resolution follows this precedence, each field decided independently:
.IP \(bu 3
An explicit
.I playbook
or
.I role
argument always wins over history.
.IP \(bu 3
Explicit
.B \-\-tags
/
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-\-skip\-tags ,
or
.B \-\-limit
/
.BR \-l ,
if given, win over that target's own history, field by field \(em
otherwise the history entry's own tags/skip\-tags/hosts pre\-fill the
dialog.
.IP \(bu 3
Any
.I other
passthrough argument given on the command line (inventory,
extra\-vars, verbosity, \&...) replaces the history entry's own
remembered arguments outright, rather than merging with them.
.IP \(bu 3
With no target given at all,
.B rerun
falls back to whichever playbook or role was invoked most recently, by
any verb. If nothing has ever been recorded for this project,
.B rerun
exits with a usage error.
.PP
A target with no recorded history at all (never run before) still
works \(em every dialog field simply starts empty.
.PP
The dialog itself offers
.B "Start with play"
and
.B "Start with task"
fields, both autocompleted from
.IR playbook.yml \(aqs
own top\-level play/task names \(em see
.BR tangsible-run (1)'s
STARTING PARTWAY THROUGH section for what each does.
.B \-\-start\-at\-play
.I name
on the
.B rerun
command line pre\-fills the dialog's
.B "Start with play"
field, the same way
.B \-\-tags
/
.B \-\-limit
pre\-fill Tags/Hosts above \(em but unlike those, it is extracted
.I before
the precedence rules above ever see it, is never itself part of the
target's recorded history, and a bad play name is not caught until the
dialog is confirmed (exactly as if it had been typed into the field by
hand), rather than up front the way
.BR tangsible-run (1)
checks it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Same as
.BR tangsible-run (1);
see
.BR tangsible (1).
Resolving no target at all (see above) is a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tangsible (1),
.BR tangsible-run (1),
.BR tangsible-role (1)
